      "writtenOn": "2019-03-05T21:44:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, maybe my idea to use \u0027keyslot \u003c 0\u0027 to represent \"no encryption\" isn\u0027t best, because now it means the keyslot has to be explicitly initialized rather than just left 0.  That adds some  complexity and possibilities for mistakes, and it seems to break the existing conventions with struct bio.\n\nHow about instead using a request flag (not a \"bio flag\") to indicate that inline encryption is enabled?  See \u0027enum req_flag_bits\u0027 in include/linux/blk_types.h.  You could add a flag __REQ_INLINECRYPT.\n\n[As I mentioned earlier, it\u0027s also possible that people will want a pointer to a separately allocated struct instead, in which case encryption disabled would just be represented by NULL.  I\u0027m unsure whether we\u0027ll need to go there though.]",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 325,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 325,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "fc8a42830e382302731d7eeb887396bcfccb0a02",
